# Accepted CVEs — every entry needs a REASON and an EXPIRY.
#
# Only for findings with NO upstream fix. Anything with a patched release gets
# upgraded instead; see the [tool.uv] override-dependencies block in
# pyproject.toml for the ones that needed a pin lifted.
#
# Re-check on expiry: `trivy fs --scanners vuln .` and drop any line whose
# upstream has since shipped a fix.

# ── aider-chat 0.86.2 ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 0.86.2 IS the latest release; there is no version to move to. We depend on
# aider for exactly ONE thing: its bundled tree-sitter tags queries, via
# `aider.repomap.RepoMap.get_tags_raw` (see indexing/treesitter_ingest). That
# path parses source files locally and touches none of the code these CVEs are
# in. Nothing in AIForge invokes aider's editor, its LLM client, or its cache.
#
# Code injection in `editor_coder.run` — we never call the editor coder.
CVE-2026-10175 exp:2026-11-30
# SSRF via the AWS EC2 metadata endpoint — reached from aider's own model/
# network stack, which we do not use (AIForge has its own llm.client).
CVE-2026-10177 exp:2026-11-30

# ── diskcache 5.6.3 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Transitive, via aider-chat only. No fixed version exists upstream.
# Arbitrary code execution through insecure pickle deserialisation — reachable
# only by loading a cache someone else wrote. We never construct aider's cache:
# `RepoMap(root=tempfile.mkdtemp(...))` points it at a fresh throwaway dir on
# every process, precisely so no real repo's cache is read or written.
CVE-2025-69872 exp:2026-11-30

# NOTE. The durable fix for all three is to drop aider-chat and read the
# tags queries directly (we already depend on tree-sitter-language-pack and
# grep-ast). That removes these CVEs AND the importlib-metadata override, but
# it means reimplementing the generic tags engine, so it is a deliberate piece
# of work rather than a scanner-driven one.
